Black Friday falls on 27 November 2026 and Cyber Monday on 30 November. A sale banner does not tell you whether a price is unusual, so it helps to check before you pay. Google published a holiday shopping guide on 16 November that points to four tools. This how-to turns that guide and Google's help pages into a short routine, and flags where each feature is limited by country or settings.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Google competes with Anthropic. Stackbrook did not test any of these features and has no affiliate relationship with Google or any retailer mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.*

## Check what is available to you first

The tools do not share one set of rules. Google Shopping Help lists price tracking in the United States, Canada, Australia, Japan and India, and warns that updates are rolling out, so some features may be missing on some devices or Google surfaces. The Universal Cart help page says the cart is currently available only in the United States.

![Table of Google shopping tools showing where each is listed as available and what the help page says you need](/images/site1/black-friday-2026-google-price-tracking-how-to-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

If you live outside the listed countries, do not count on these alerts. Check the retailer's own wish list or email alerts instead.

## Step 1: Compare merchants and price history

Google's guide says you can tap a product listing on Google to compare prices across merchants. The same view shows price history, so you can see whether the current price counts as low, typical or high.

Treat that label as one signal. Before deciding, check that the listings you are comparing are the same model, size and colour. Then add delivery charges and read the return terms on the merchant's site. The help page also reminds shoppers that prices are dynamic and can change.

## Step 2: Set a price alert

Google Shopping Help says price tracking works when you are signed in to your Google Account, on the web or in the Google app. It also asks you to have notifications turned on. The steps it gives are:

1. Search for a product or a product category.
2. Open or scroll to a product card.
3. Tap **Track price**.
4. If there are options such as size or colour, pick the ones you want. You can also choose a target price.
5. Tap **OK** to confirm.

When the price drops below your target, the help page says you get a push notification through the Google app and an email. Google may also alert you to price drops on other products your activity suggests you are interested in. The help page points to your notification settings for turning those off. Google's holiday guide adds another route: ask AI Mode in Search to let you know when there is a deal.

To stop tracking in the mobile app, open your profile picture or initial, then Saves & Collections, then All saved items. Select the items, tap the trash icon and confirm removal. Price tracking emails also carry a link to unsubscribe from a single product.

## Step 3: Use Chrome if you browse there

Chrome has its own price insights and tracking. The steps here are for Chrome on Android, and other devices may differ. Its Android help page sets four conditions: you live in Australia, Canada, India, Japan or the US, you are signed in to Chrome with a Google Account, you opt in to syncing tabs and history, and the setting called "Make searches and browsing better" is on. Think about whether you want to share that much browsing data before switching these on.

On a product page, the help page's route to price history is to tap "Price is low" in the address bar, where you can also choose to track the item. If you do not see that label, try More, then Track prices. Tracked items appear under Bookmarks in a folder called Tracked products. The same page describes a discount badge for US users that lets you copy a code. If no expiry date is shown, it says the code is valid for only 24 hours.

## Step 4: Gather items in Universal Cart (US)

Google announced on 15 October that Universal Cart was rolling out in the United States across Search and the Gemini app. It lets you save products from different retailers in one place. Google says it sends notices about price drops and restocks, shows relevant promo codes, and uses Google Wallet to help you choose between payment methods based on card perks. Check your card issuer's terms before relying on a suggested perk.

There are two ways to pay. For participating merchants you can check out inside Google using Google Pay. Google's launch post gives examples including Nike, Sephora, Target, Ulta Beauty, Walmart, Wayfair and many Shopify merchants. Otherwise you can move your items to the retailer's site and finish there. The merchant remains the seller of record and provides customer support, so take order problems to the merchant.

Do not rely on a chat to manage the cart. According to the help page, Gemini Apps and AI Mode see only part of your data, so asking them what you have saved, where a parcel is, or to put something in the cart may not work. Open the cart itself instead. The My Orders tab can also have gaps, because not every retailer reports purchases back to Google.

## Know when a button can buy for you

Google Shopping Help mentions a "Buy for me with GPay" button on some tracked products. Using it lets Google's agentic checkout (software that completes a purchase on your behalf) buy from the merchant's website with Google Pay once your target price is reached. The page says you get a notification to review and approve the purchase, payment method and shipping address before any transaction proceeds. Read that screen carefully, including the total and the address, before you approve.

This feature has a narrower footprint than price tracking. The Google Pay help page says at the top that agentic checkout is currently available only in the US and Australia, in English, for select merchants that accept Google Pay. It asks for a valid payment method and a US or Australian address saved in Google Wallet. A troubleshooting section on the same page mentions only the US, so Australian shoppers should confirm in their own account. The page also says the purchase is designed to go through only if the final price is the same as or lower than the total you confirmed.

## Two extra tools for gifts and shop shelves

For gift ideas, Google's guide suggests describing the person and your budget in plain language to AI Mode or the Gemini app. It says the results can include customer reviews, photos and store availability. For shopping in person, open the Google app, tap the Lens camera icon and photograph an item to find reviews, product details and similar styles. The guide does not state country or account limits for these two, so check what your own app offers.

## A quick routine before you pay

Decide the most you are willing to pay. Check the price history, then confirm the same item at a second merchant. Look at delivery cost, delivery date and the return window. If the price is above your limit, set an alert and walk away. A deal you did not plan to buy is still money spent.
